[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0, leaf_router_0_1, super_spine_router_0
diagnosis_text:
Current reachability is OK (pc_0_0 ↔ pc_0_1 pings succeed), but your “one road” concern is valid: ECMP isn’t being used, so all east–west traffic is funneled through a single spine. Evidence:
- leaf_router_0_0 installs only one path to 10.0.1.0/24: “B>* 10.0.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.4, eth0” (toward spine_router_0_0). No parallel next-hop via 172.16.0.8 (spine_router_0_1).
- leaf_router_0_1 installs only one path to 10.0.0.0/24: “B>* 10.0.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.6, eth0” (also spine_router_0_0). Again, no equal path via 172.16.0.10 (spine_router_0_1).
- super_spine_router_0 also points both 10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24 to a single neighbor: “via 172.16.0.1, eth0,” not leveraging its second spine link (172.16.0.3).

Mechanism: FRR’s default BGP bestpath selects a single winner; equal-cost multipath across different eBGP ASNs is not enabled by default. With two spines (AS 65100 and 65101), the leaves and super-spine need explicit multipath settings to install both next-hops.

Root cause: Missing BGP ECMP configuration (maximum-paths and as-path multipath relax) on leaves and the super-spine.

Corrective fix: On leaf_router_0_0, leaf_router_0_1, and super_spine_router_0, under router bgp … address-family ipv4 unicast, enable:
- maximum-paths 2 (or higher)
- bgp bestpath as-path multipath-relax
Then perform a soft clear. You should see two next-hops installed per remote subnet, restoring load-sharing across both spines while maintaining reachability. === END ===